{
  "configuration_id": "config_002",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_001 (RNA-only, PCA n2000, single profile) achieves 48.5% accuracy, establishing RNA as the dominant modality, while config_010 (image-only, ResNet50) is very weak at 20.8%. We have no information yet on whether the multiprofile axis adds value. Question: config_002 (RNA-only, PCA n2000, multiprofile, avgpool) isolates the effect of switching from single to multiprofile while holding the RNA encoder (pca_n2000) and modality constant. Using avgpool (the simplest aggregation) keeps the comparison clean. Why this is the best next step: (1) It is a single-variable comparison directly from our best baseline, so any performance change can be attributed to the profile setting. (2) The multiprofile axis is a major design decision that underpins many downstream multimodal configs (config_018–051); knowing whether it helps with the base encoder tells us whether to invest in multiprofile+fusion or focus on single-profile improvements. (3) If multiprofile+avgpool does not improve over single-profile PCA, it signals that the profile axis may not be the bottleneck, redirecting budget toward encoder quality or fusion strategy. (4) It avoids the confound of simultaneously changing encoder, dimensionality, and profile as in config_004/005."
}

